    "knowledge_point": "Integrating a Rational Function With an Irreducible Binomial Quadratic Factor",
    "problem": "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
    "worked_solution": "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+3$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+3)(x+4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+5x-9 & =(Ax+B)(x+4)+C({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=-3,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+3| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$",
